WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:27.289
Even if you never cared for his songs, you
surely heard of him. He was a musical prodigy,

00:00:27.289 --> 00:00:32.320
the first real electric guitar hero. He grew
from humble beginnings and a fractured family

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:38.550
to establish himself as one of the most influential
guitarists of all time, a major force in 20th

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:45.970
century music. Get ready to jam out on your
air-guitar with today’s protagonist: Jimi

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:51.900
Hendrix.
Jimi, Jimmy or Johnny?

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:57.960
Jimi Hendrix was born Johnny Allen Hendrix
on November 27, 1942 in Seattle. His father

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.989
Al, later renamed him James Marshall, or Jimmy
for short.

00:01:01.989 --> 00:01:06.480
Young Jimmy descended from a family of professional
entertainers. His grandmother, Nora Rose,

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.750
was a dancer with a traveling vaudeville troupe
called ‘Lacy’s Band’, and her husband

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.890
Bertran was a stagehand and roadie for the
same outfit.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:19.230
By 1912, the travelling couple had settled
in Vancouver, Canada and in 1919, they had

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:25.100
their second born, James Allen, or ‘Al’.
Young Al had inherited from Nora a love of

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:30.810
music and competitive dancing. In 1941 Al
got engaged to Lucille Jeter, a lively and

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:35.830
beautiful 16-year-old from Roslyn, Washington.
They had met that very same year at a concert

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:40.600
featuring legendary Jazz pianist Fats Waller.
They were married the following year, and

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:45.710
as we already know, welcomed Johnny slash
Jimmy on the 27th of November.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:50.810
Is it important to note that Jimi Hendrix
was named ‘Johnny’ at birth? Yes, it is,

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:55.490
because it was a symptom of Al and Lucille’s
fast-deteriorating relationship. Since their

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:00.860
engagement days, Lucille had never let go
of what she loved the most: partying and enjoying

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:06.020
the nightlife. And she did party hard, eventually
developing an alcohol problem and psychiatric

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:11.180
issues. Lucille also had developed a reputation
for being unfaithful. Amongst her alleged

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:16.620
lovers: one Johnny Williams.
So, when Johnny/Jimmy was born, Al was stationed

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:21.260
in the Pacific with the US Army. But at the
end of WWII, when Al returned to Seattle from

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:26.620
his service, he legally changed Johnny’s
name. Was Jimmy an illegitimate child? That,

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:31.109
we don’t know, and neither did Al, but at
least he wanted to erase any possible link

00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:36.370
to a potential extramarital affair.
By 1947, young Jimmy’s domestic life could

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:41.900
be accurately described as dysfunctional.
Lucille was by all accounts a caring and loving

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:46.830
mother... when she was there. Unfortunately,
that was a rare occurrence. Her drinking problem

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:51.480
worsened, and she developed a penchant for
simply disappearing for days at the time.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:57.280
Naturally, this disappearing act led to frequent,
violent rows between her and Al. But the couple

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:02.660
stuck together four more years, and had even
more children together: Leon, Joseph, Cathy

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:07.260
and finally Pamela, in 1951.
In December of that same year, Al and Lucille

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.480
finally separated, and Al took over custody
of his children.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.330
Money was always tight for the single father.
Joseph, Cathy and Pamela were all assigned

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:19.180
to foster families. Al took on multiple jobs
-- janitor, gas station attendant, gardener

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:23.420
-- which allowed him to look after Jimmy and
Leon. But even so, the two brothers spent

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:27.980
much of the early 1950s under the care of
relatives, friends, or neighbours. Eventually,

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:32.139
Al was forced to foster his eldest sons, too,
for a brief period.

00:03:32.139 --> 00:03:36.519
In 1953, Al was able to eventually stabilise
his financial situation, and he was granted

00:03:36.519 --> 00:03:41.740
custody of Jimmy once more.
A – D - Em

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:50.829
Al and Jimmy’s relationship may have been
close, or tense, or both, varying on accounts.

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:55.609
They surely did bond over music. From an early
age, Jimmy developed a fascination for legendary

00:03:55.609 --> 00:04:00.930
Blues guitarists and vocalists in Al’s record
collection. Artists like B.B. King, Muddy

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:05.050
Waters, Howlin’ Wolf, and, most of all,
the legendary Robert Johnson, who had learned

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:09.359
his guitar skills after signing a covenant
with the Devil at a crossroads; whose fingerplay

00:04:09.359 --> 00:04:14.870
was so skilful that he sounded like two guitarists
playing at once; and who died at 27 after

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:18.769
drinking a shot of poisoned whiskey.
This fascination for Blues guitar would soon

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:23.449
be noticed by Al, with the help of a broom.
You see, Al Hendrix ran a tight ship around

00:04:23.449 --> 00:04:27.760
the home, and while he was out, he required
Jimmy to keep his bedroom tidy and clean.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:32.259
When he returned home, he would often notice
some broom straw on Jimmy’s bedroom floor.

00:04:32.259 --> 00:04:36.050
In Al’s version of the story, he discovered
that Jimmy would sit on his bed, listening

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:41.590
to Blues tracks while strumming the broom
as if it were a guitar. Moved by this passion,

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:46.230
Al got Jimmy his first instrument: an old
ukulele with one string.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:50.349
In another, slightly less warm version of
events, Al would beat Jimmy senseless every

00:04:50.349 --> 00:04:53.539
time he returned home to find straw on the
floor.

00:04:53.539 --> 00:04:58.259
Whatever the truth about their relationship,
Al did encourage his son’s musical talent.

00:04:58.259 --> 00:05:04.460
In 1957, Al allowed Jimmy to graduate from
one-string uke to a full six-stringed acoustic

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:08.960
guitar he had bought for five dollars.
Jimmy had a keen musical ear and immediately

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:13.400
learned how to tune his guitar and play songs.
“Smoke on the Water” was still to come,

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.300
so the first tune of choice for a novice guitarist
like Jimmy was “Louie Louie” by Richard

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:25.199
Berry, with its three chords: A, D, E minor.
In the summer of 1958, Jimmy joined his first

00:05:25.199 --> 00:05:29.819
band, The Velvetones. The following year,
Al got him his first electric guitar, a Supro

00:05:29.819 --> 00:05:36.490
Ozark 1560S: a simple model with a single
pick-up coil and an $89 price tag. By going

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:39.759
electric, Jimmy and the Velvetones could play
their first concert.

00:05:39.759 --> 00:05:43.569
Even though they were too young for the nightclub
circuit, Jimmy could still hone his skills

00:05:43.569 --> 00:05:50.080
playing teen dances in recreation halls and
housing projects. And hone he did -- so much

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:55.069
so that he had his first admirers, and the
Velvetones included in their dance sets his

00:05:55.069 --> 00:06:00.440
original tune, “Jimmy’s Blues”.
Jimmy’s reputation grew, and in 1959, he

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:05.270
was invited to join another band, the Rocking
Kings. But he felt trapped in a small scene

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:11.129
of house parties and recreation centres, already
aware of his potential and the unique sound

00:06:11.129 --> 00:06:14.310
he was able to create.
Even at this early stage, the sound of his

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:19.289
electric guitar had a unique quality to it,
thanks to a very practical reason -- as a

00:06:19.289 --> 00:06:23.639
left-handed musician, Jimmy could not afford
to buy a left-handed guitar, so he had to

00:06:23.639 --> 00:06:28.639
make do with 2nd hand standard guitars for
righties. He made everything work by swapping

00:06:28.639 --> 00:06:33.310
the position of the strings, but he did not
want to (or perhaps wasn’t able to) flip

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:38.099
the position of the coils, or pick-ups.
The result? The part of the coil designed

00:06:38.099 --> 00:06:42.120
to pick up the notes from the ‘chunky’
strings was now amplifying the vibrations

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:47.120
from the thin strings, and vice versa. The
result was a signature mix of bright high

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:51.650
notes and delicate low ones.
An opportunity arose for young Jimi in 1960

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:56.949
when he was invited to join James Thomas and
His Tomcats. This was a professionally managed

00:06:56.949 --> 00:07:01.340
band, specialised in higher-end venues, like
military officers’ clubs.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:05.770
Soon, Jimi would be spending more time with
the officers.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:12.499
Joyriding
Jimmy’s childhood and household had been

00:07:12.499 --> 00:07:17.889
unstable and fractured, only slightly mitigated
by Al’s strict discipline. The end result?

00:07:17.889 --> 00:07:21.900
Jimmy knew how to get into trouble.
By 1960, Jimmy had dropped out of Garfield

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.509
High School. According to him, he had been
expelled for ‘smart-mouthing’ a teacher.

00:07:25.509 --> 00:07:29.850
In between gigs and rehearsals Jimmy, was
helping Al with his new landscaping business

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:34.199
to make a few extra buck, but it was never
enough to cover Jimmy’s taste for expensive

00:07:34.199 --> 00:07:39.300
and flashy clothing. According to some accounts,
he wasn’t afraid to steal to fund his lifestyle.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:44.479
At minimum, Jimmy committed two felonies that
we know about. On May 2, 1961, he took a car

00:07:44.479 --> 00:07:48.710
without the owner’s permission and went
on a joy ride. He did it again, four days

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:51.710
later, though it’s not clear if it was the
same car.

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:55.740
At the end of the month, Hendrix joined the
Army – probably as an alternative to serving

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:59.740
a jail sentence.
Later photos of Hendrix in old-fashioned,

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:05.499
Victorian Army tunics are iconic. So it’s
kind of strange to see Jimmy in standard US

00:08:05.499 --> 00:08:09.909
Army olive fatigues!
In June of 1961, Private Hendrix was dropping

00:08:09.909 --> 00:08:14.580
push ups at Fort Ord, California, before being
shipped to Fort Campbell, Kentucky, training

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:19.979
with the 101st Airborne Division. It was only
at this stage that Al sent him his new guitar,

00:08:19.979 --> 00:08:25.009
a Danelectro Bronze Standard nicknamed ‘Betty
Jean’, after his fiancée at that time.

00:08:25.009 --> 00:08:29.469
Jimmy was not the only musician at Fort Campbell.
Billy Cox, a bass player, was able to spot

00:08:29.469 --> 00:08:31.389
Jimmy’s talent, which he later described
as

00:08:31.389 --> 00:08:37.330
“coming from a creative musical space somewhere
between Beethoven and John Lee Hooker”

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:42.460
Cox and Hendrix hit it off and formed a new
band, the King Kasuals. Jimmy and Billy had

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:47.080
the potential, but the Kasuals never went
far, as Jimmy, burdened by his constant cash

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:50.990
flow problems, would frequently pawn his guitar
for a few bucks.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:55.630
Jimmy was a source of frustration also for
his superiors, described as being

00:08:55.630 --> 00:09:00.690
"unable to conform to military rules and regulations"
In later accounts, Hendrix reported that he

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:05.520
had been honourably discharged from the Army
because he had broken his ankle during a parachute-training

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:12.810
jump. But no, that’s BS. On July 2, 1962,
Jimmy was discharged with an ‘Undesirable’

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:16.890
status. Nothing too dramatic: his officers
simply noted that he was severely distracted

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:20.870
“while performing duties, due to excessive
thinking about his guitar.”

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:25.780
I wonder if that Sergeant realized he had
just written Jimmy Hendrix’s first recorded

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:28.901
review!
In addition to that appraisal, other Army

00:09:28.901 --> 00:09:33.890
medical documents report Jimmy’s declaration
that he was gay – a common stratagem used

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:39.030
at the time to be discharged early form Service.
After his discharge, Jimmy left Fort Campbell

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:44.640
with $400 in his pocket, after selling Betty
Jean to an Army buddy – the guitar, not

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:49.350
his girlfriend. He collected his final Army
wages and moved to Clarksville, Tennessee,

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:52.550
settling around a Jazz Bar that he liked.
A lot.

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:58.190
He liked it so much that he spent $384 in
one night. That’s almost $3,300 in today’s

00:09:58.190 --> 00:10:00.940
money.
Stranded in Tennessee, all Jimmy could do

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:05.970
was return to Fort Campbell and beg his friend
to return him the Danelectro. With his trusty

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:10.510
six-strings, Jimmy was able to make a few
bucks playing solo gigs and sustain himself

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:15.060
until Billy Cox finished his Service in September.
Two things of note here:

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:19.300
One: it seems like Jimmy was in no rush to
return to Betty Jean – the girlfriend that

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:23.620
is, not the guitar.
Two: there are no recorded traces of the Danelectro

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:29.350
after September of 1962, so it may still be
in Clarksville … if you hail from there,

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:36.900
start looking for it now, as it could fetch
you a $100,000 at auction.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:42.370
Playing to the Stars
By September of 1962, bass player Billy Cox

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:47.150
had also left the Army. Billy and Jimmy reassembled
the King Kasuals and set their sights on one

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:52.950
of the Capital cities of American music, Nashville.
Very soon, the Kasuals became the Kings of

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:57.560
the black nightclub scene in Nashville, supporting
touring soul stars like the Marvelettes or

00:10:57.560 --> 00:11:00.841
Curtis Mayfield.
Things were looking up for Jimmy and he was

00:11:00.841 --> 00:11:05.090
even able to afford train tickets to visit
Grandma Nora – the vaudeville dancer – in

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:09.760
Vancouver. This happened around Christmas
of 1963: during his stay in Canada, Hendrix

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:14.610
joined a local band for a short tour, which
earned him the attention of early rock ‘n’

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:18.290
roll icon Little Richard.
Little Richard hired him as a session guitarist

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:22.600
in his band, touring and recording with the
legend. He can be heard on the single “I

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:24.850
Don’t Know What You’ve Got, But It’s
Got Me”.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:30.390
During 1964 and 1965, Jimmy became a sought-after
session musician, playing recording studios

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:35.490
from New York to Los Angeles. One of his best-known
records of the time is ‘Testify’ by the

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:40.920
Isley Brothers, an early, energetic funk piece,
where Jimmy first displays his lead guitar

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:44.480
chops on vinyl.
During this period Hendrix became infatuated

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:49.380
with Lithofayne Pridgon, a former lover of
King of Soul Sam Cooke, who was well connected

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:55.380
to the New York black music scene. The infatuation
was reciprocal, with Lithofayne, or ‘Faye’,

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:59.000
describing him as
“… skinny, raw-boned, over-f**ked, underfed-looking

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:03.020
… Hendrix was my type”
Jimmy and Faye had a passionate affair, in

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:07.850
which Pridgon embodied the role of both muse
and mentor, introducing him to influential

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:12.600
Soul and R&amp;B musicians. However, from her
point of view, they were never really a couple,

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:17.820
and certainly not in an exclusive relationship.
Jimmy was insanely jealous of her, but eventually

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:23.000
had to accept the fact that this ‘cute little
heartbreaker’ could never be all his …

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:28.200
In late 1965, Jimmy had settled in the Greenwich
Village district of New York. He had formed

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:33.440
a new ensemble, Jimmy James and the Blue Flames,
with whom he played in various nightclubs.

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:37.680
It was at the Cheetah Club that in 1966 he
was spotted by a British model called Linda

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:42.120
Keith – the girlfriend of Rolling Stones
guitarist Keith Richards. Linda invited Jimmy

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:46.500
to a party and discovered that, though he
may have displayed the bravado and confidence

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:51.910
of a guitar hero while on the stage, regular
ol’ Jimmy was shy, polite, afflicted by

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:56.751
low self-esteem, and in need of a good meal.
Linda immediately realised Jimmy’s star

00:12:56.751 --> 00:13:01.270
potential and badly wanted to help him. She
loaned him one of Keith Richard’s guitars,

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:05.980
a white Fender Stratocaster.
She then invited a series of influential managers

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:11.140
and producers to listen to Hendrix play. First,
it was Andrew Oldham, manager for the Rolling

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:15.760
Stones. But that night, Jimmy’s performance
was lacklustre. Then, it was producer Seymour

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:20.190
Stein, who later discovered Madonna. This
time, Jimmy was in much better form: overcome

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:25.300
with enthusiasm and sheer rock bad-assery,
the guitarist smashed Keith Richards’white

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:30.600
Fender onto the stage. Linda was so furious
with Jimmy that Stein fled the club, not keen

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:34.850
on getting involved in the row.
Third time was the lucky one, when Linda invited

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:39.500
Chas Chandler to attend yet another gig at
‘Café Wha?’. Chandler was the bass player

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:44.300
with The Animals, now turned manager. He agreed
with Linda that Jimmy was a full-on rock star

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:48.410
just waiting to be launched.
Chas convinced Jimmy to fly with him to London

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:52.530
where he would quit the Animals and manage
the guitarist’s budding career.

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:59.070
On September 23, 1966, the bassist and the
guitarist took off from New York, headed to

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:04.180
England. It was mid-flight that Chas proposed
a new spelling for his client’s stage name.

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:41.750
Jimi Hendrix was born.
Are you Experienced?

00:14:41.750 --> 00:15:54.810
Once in London, Chandler organised auditions
to form Jimi’s band, settling on a ‘power

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:59.890
trio’ format, whereby Jimi would cover both
guitar and lead vocal duties. The slots were

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:04.810
filled by guitarist Noel Redding – who was
reluctantly put on bass -- and a whirlwind

00:16:04.810 --> 00:16:08.790
of a drummer called Mitch Mitchell.
Chandler also enlisted help from The Animals’

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:13.680
own manager, Michael Jeffery as he was more
business-savvy. Jeffery was later described

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:18.780
as a sketchy type, with alleged ties to organised
crimes in Newcastle, Northern England, and

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:22.970
even to the secret services.
Under Jeffery’s guidance, the band was christened

00:16:22.970 --> 00:16:27.260
‘The Jimi Hendrix Experience’, and immediately
set to work on a record.

00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:31.250
Before the album could be released, Jimi was,
as ever, strapped for cash and in need of

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:36.060
playing small gigs … it was at one of these
semi-improvised sessions that Chandler introduced

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:40.980
him to DJ and hairdresser Kathy Etchingham.
It took Jimi one pick-up line:

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:43.790
“I think you are beautiful.”
And a couple of hours later they were spending

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:48.030
the night in Jimi’s hotel. The next morning,
they were awakened by another of Jimi’s

00:16:48.030 --> 00:16:52.110
many lovers. Mad with jealousy, the unnamed
lady threatened to smash Hendrix’s only

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:54.060
guitar … on his head.
Editor’s note: Simon, you’ll want to hit

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:55.060
the end of this next paragraph with a bit
of sass, though not an overwhelming amount.

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:56.060
It’s not intended as straightforward sarcasm,
but there is a payoff coming later. Leaving

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:57.060
you a note because it hit my eyes funny the
first time I read it.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:58.530
The early morning drama did not deter Kathy,
who became Jimi’s regular girlfriend in

00:16:58.530 --> 00:17:03.420
London. She stuck by him during the recording
sessions of The Experience’s debut album,

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:09.039
while they lead a simple and quiet life, playing
Monopoly and Twister and eating mashed potatoes.

00:17:09.039 --> 00:17:13.721
Jimi, Noel and Mitch released their debut
single: a cover of “Hey Joe”, a track

00:17:13.721 --> 00:17:18.319
that deserves its own Biographics episode.
The next single was an original Hendrix tune,

00:17:18.319 --> 00:17:22.939
one that would introduce the world to his
pyrotechnic lead guitar as well as songwriting

00:17:22.939 --> 00:17:26.899
talent: “Purple Haze”
The success of these singles granted The Jimi

00:17:26.899 --> 00:17:31.419
Hendrix Experience a slot at the Monterey
International Pop Festival in California.

00:17:31.419 --> 00:17:37.389
The power trio took to the stage on June 18,
1967, unleashing on the audience a barrage

00:17:37.389 --> 00:17:42.799
of original tunes and covers that literally
set the stage on fire. Rolling Stone wrote:

00:17:42.799 --> 00:17:48.190
“ … an orgasmic explosion of singing feedback,
agitated stretches of jazzy improvisation

00:17:48.190 --> 00:17:53.480
and recombinant R&amp;B guitar … Hendrix, egged
on by Noel Redding and Mitch Mitchell's rhythmic

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:58.050
frenzy, drove "Wild Thing" head-on into a
wall of white noise”

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:03.279
At the end of a heavily distorted rendition
of Wild Thing by The Troggs, Jimi set his

00:18:03.279 --> 00:18:06.200
guitar on fire, before smashing it to bits
onto the stage.

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:10.649
The Jimi Hendrix Experience would be impossible
to ignore.

00:18:10.649 --> 00:18:17.830
Yes, I am
The first LP of the trio, Are You Experienced?,

00:18:17.830 --> 00:18:22.940
hit the record stores in August of 1967.
The music critics’ initial response was

00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:27.360
unenthusiastic. This is how Rolling Stone
summarised their review of the album:

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:31.330
“Despite Jimi’s musical brilliance and
the group’s total precision, the poor quality

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:35.279
of the songs, and the inanity of the lyrics,
too often get in the way …

00:18:35.279 --> 00:18:41.370
Above all, this record is unrelentingly violent,
and lyrically, inartistically violent at that”

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:47.110
Not everybody agreed. Many appreciated Jimi’s
alchemist brew of psychedelia, 12-bar blues,

00:18:47.110 --> 00:18:51.720
hard rock and aggressive funk. Are You Experienced?
went on to spend 106 weeks on the Billboard

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:55.880
200, eventually selling more than five million
copies in the U.S. alone.

00:18:55.880 --> 00:19:00.840
Jimi’s debut was packed with many of his
greatest works, songs impossible to ignore

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:05.190
even if you don’t really ‘dig’ Hendrix.
Hey Joe! Purple Haze! Manic Depression! The

00:19:05.190 --> 00:19:08.500
Wind Cries Mary! Red House! Personally, I’m
partial to Foxey Lady.

00:19:08.500 --> 00:19:12.780
Jimi Hendrix is routinely praised for his
lead guitar work -- his imaginative, fast,

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:17.639
lengthy solos. But Foxey Lady is a clear example
of Jimi’s powerful rhythm guitar playing;

00:19:17.639 --> 00:19:21.240
in other words, the chords and riffs that
make the backbone of a song, and the hallmark

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:25.639
of a good songwriter. The main riff of Foxey
Lady, heard in the verses and during the solo

00:19:25.639 --> 00:19:31.240
section, is deceptively simple: it alternates
a low F sharp with a high E and A, creating

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:36.320
a funky, bouncy effect. But it revolves around
a complex chord: ‘F Sharp seventh to the

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:40.240
ninth’ – a chord so distinctive that it
became known as ‘The Hendrix Chord’.

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:44.580
Lyrically speaking, the song is about one
“cute little heart breaker … a sweet little

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:46.850
love maker”
A lady who, Jimi says

00:19:46.850 --> 00:19:51.250
“Got to be all mine, all mine”.
Although never confirmed by Hendrix himself,

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:56.380
the Foxey Lady is almost certainly his lover
from the early New York days, Faye Pidgon.

00:19:56.380 --> 00:19:59.470
Or was she?
Another obvious candidate is Kathy Etchingham,

00:19:59.470 --> 00:20:03.830
who claims to have also inspired Jimi’s
‘The Wind Cries Mary’. One night, Jimi

00:20:03.830 --> 00:20:08.240
accused Kathy’s mash of being too lumpy.
The couple had a furious row, ending with

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:13.260
Kathy leaving their apartment. Jimi, saddened
by her departure, composed his most famous

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:20.059
ballad.
Undesirable people

00:20:20.059 --> 00:20:24.820
The couple reconciled almost immediately and
decided to go steady from then on, throughout

00:20:24.820 --> 00:20:29.610
1967 and until the end of 1968. But their
love was still fragile, threatened on all

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:33.899
sides by the traps of fame.
After the success of Are You Experienced,

00:20:33.899 --> 00:20:37.999
Hendrix was a global superstar, with all its
benefits and added pressures. His trio was

00:20:37.999 --> 00:20:42.370
expected to tour and record constantly, abiding
to an exhausting schedule.

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:47.100
In early January, the Experience was touring
Sweden, when on the 10th of January, Jimi

00:20:47.100 --> 00:20:52.149
was reported checking in at Gothenburg police
headquarters, apparently on charges of possession

00:20:52.149 --> 00:20:56.840
of illegal substances. It was clear to everybody
in his entourage that Hendrix was not stranger

00:20:56.840 --> 00:21:04.019
to illicit drugs, but in 1968, his consumption
escalated from marijuana to LSD, amphetamines

00:21:04.019 --> 00:21:07.169
and heroin.
Kathy noted how during this period Jimi’s

00:21:07.169 --> 00:21:12.240
normally sweet character started to change,
becoming aggressive with his friends and even

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:16.050
smashing up hotel rooms. According to Kathy,
Jimi’s entourage was infiltrated by

00:21:16.050 --> 00:21:19.869
‘dodgy, undesirable people, with an aggressive
edge to them’

00:21:19.869 --> 00:21:24.860
Scroungers at best, and possibly drug dealers
or criminal types. Kathy described how

00:21:24.860 --> 00:21:29.440
"He started to look really rough. His hair
was breaking off, he didn't have healthy skin,

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:33.130
he looked as if he'd aged 10 years in two
and a half."

00:21:33.130 --> 00:21:37.179
Kathy Etchingham decided she had to move on,
and the couple split up in 1969.

00:21:37.179 --> 00:21:41.029
All the drama in the background did not prevent
The Experience from delivering two albums

00:21:41.029 --> 00:21:47.169
in 1968: Axis: Bold as Love and Electric Ladyland.
Rolling Stone, via reviewer Jim Miller, continued

00:21:47.169 --> 00:21:50.929
to show its aversion for Jimi. While Miller
acknowledges the album as a

00:21:50.929 --> 00:21:54.590
‘vortex of apocalyptic transcendence’
He writes off the songs as

00:21:54.590 --> 00:21:55.590
‘basically, a bore’.
If I am allowed an opinion, I beg to disagree,

00:21:55.590 --> 00:21:56.590
While Axis is not packed with classics as
its predecessor, it showcases Jimi’s evolution

00:21:56.590 --> 00:21:57.590
and experimentation. This is the album in
which he perfected the use of the novel ‘Wah

00:21:57.590 --> 00:21:58.590
wah’ pedal; in which he toyed with recording
techniques, such as performing a solo from

00:21:58.590 --> 00:21:59.590
the last note to the first, and then playing
the tape backwards. This can be heard in ‘Castles

00:21:59.590 --> 00:22:00.590
made of sand’, a lesser known track, but
arguably his most heartfelt one.

00:22:00.590 --> 00:22:01.590
The follow-up record, Electric Ladyland, is
widely considered Jimi’s masterpiece. Even

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:06.090
Rolling Stone was more generous in its review!
The album is noteworthy because of its production

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:11.659
history: several rock stars of the era collaborated
in it, from Stevie Winwood to Brian Jones,

00:22:11.659 --> 00:22:16.480
while Hendrix himself continued his experiments
with recording technology, using looped tapes

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:19.869
and phasing effects.
Hendrix actually left the recording studio

00:22:19.869 --> 00:22:24.240
unsatisfied with the final sound of the recording.
He was even less impressed with the cover

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:28.950
imposed by the label, a photo featuring a
group of naked women which was banned from

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:33.700
many record stores. Jimi’s initial pick
was a photo of him and the band playing with

00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:37.660
some children, taken by Linda Eastman, soon
to become Mrs Paul McCartney.

00:22:37.660 --> 00:22:43.250
Song-wise, there is a lot to choose from.
If I must pick just two tracks, they got to

00:22:43.250 --> 00:22:44.250
be ‘All along the watchtower’, a Bob Dylan
cover, which the folk singer himself admitted

00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:45.250
superior to the original. And ‘Voodoo Child
– slight return’, improvised on the cuff

00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:46.250
by the band to the benefit of a BBC documentary
crew. This may be considered Jimi’s heaviest

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:47.250
song, an ideal gateway from 1960s rock to
early heavy metal.

00:22:47.250 --> 00:22:48.250
Summer of ‘69
The Experience immediately started touring

00:22:48.250 --> 00:22:50.320
to promote Ladyland, amidst growing tensions
fuelled by Jimi’s addiction problems and

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:54.710
Redding’s growing dissatisfaction with his
bass guitar duties. And yet, they were still

00:22:54.710 --> 00:23:01.341
wildly successful. On March 22, 1969, Billboard
magazine reported them winning the 1969 Popular

00:23:01.341 --> 00:23:06.470
Music Prize, awarded at the Festival du Son,
in Paris. The same issue listed The Experience

00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:11.539
as the 2nd best selling band in the US, bested
only by Simon &amp; Garfunkel.

00:23:11.539 --> 00:23:16.539
But trouble was always behind the corner:
after a tour in Canada in May, Jimi was tried

00:23:16.539 --> 00:23:21.639
and fined for possession of narcotics in Toronto.
Shortly afterward, on the 29th of June, Noel

00:23:21.639 --> 00:23:26.649
Redding decided to call it quits after a show
in Denver. The Jimi Hendrix Experience had

00:23:26.649 --> 00:23:30.321
officially disbanded.
The break-up did not deter Jimi. He got back

00:23:30.321 --> 00:23:34.740
in touch with his old army buddy, bassist
Billy Cox. With the trusty Mitch Mitchell

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:40.890
on drums, guitarist Jerry Velez and percussionist
Juma Sultan, they assembled a new band, Gypsy

00:23:40.890 --> 00:23:47.679
Sun &amp; Rainbows. The new combo ravished audiences
and ravaged a few eardrums at possibly THE

00:23:47.679 --> 00:23:51.169
rock concert par excellence, the Woodstock
Festival.

00:23:51.169 --> 00:23:56.230
On the evening of August 18, 1969, Jimi and
friends took to the stage performing classics

00:23:56.230 --> 00:23:59.999
and new tracks alike. Even if you are not
into this kind of music, you probably have

00:23:59.999 --> 00:24:04.989
heard Jimi’s rendition of the “Star Spangled
Banner”. This mangled, electrocuted version

00:24:04.989 --> 00:24:10.370
of the US national anthem could be considered
Jimi’s most political moment, a sonic commentary

00:24:10.370 --> 00:24:16.570
on a nation divided by civil unrest and the
debate on the Vietnam War.

00:24:16.570 --> 00:24:19.820
Shortly after Woodstock, Jimi was involved
in one of the most puzzling events of his

00:24:19.820 --> 00:24:24.529
life. The story comes from drug dealer and
mobster Jon Roberts, one of many unsavoury

00:24:24.529 --> 00:24:28.499
friends of Hendrix in New York. One night,
Jimi had ventured into town looking to buy

00:24:28.499 --> 00:24:34.080
some cocaine. As he entered a club frequented
by dealers, he was spotted by two italian-american

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:39.399
young men, described by Roberts as ‘wise-guys
wannabes’. The two saw an occasion to make

00:24:39.399 --> 00:24:43.399
some serious money … and they kidnapped
the rockstar! They held him in captivity for

00:24:43.399 --> 00:24:48.259
two days, during which they contacted his
management asking for either a ransom in cash

00:24:48.259 --> 00:24:53.039
or a stake in royalties from future records.
In one version of the event, manager Michael

00:24:53.039 --> 00:24:58.610
Jeffery sent a group of thugs armed with machine
guns to free Jimi. In a less spectacular account,

00:24:58.610 --> 00:25:02.830
it was Roberts who solved the case. Using
his underworld contacts, he quickly identified

00:25:02.830 --> 00:25:05.320
the two kidnappers and threatened them over
the phone:

00:25:05.320 --> 00:25:10.029
“You let Jimi go, or you are dead. Do not
harm a hair of his Afro.”

00:25:10.029 --> 00:25:14.330
The two ‘wise guys’ relented, and a week
later Roberts retaliated by giving them the

00:25:14.330 --> 00:25:18.010
beating of their life.
Apparently, Jimi had been so stoned he barely

00:25:18.010 --> 00:25:22.529
took notice of the whole ordeal.
Jeffery insisted he get back to work immediately,

00:25:22.529 --> 00:25:27.730
but not with the Gypsy Sun and Rainbows. Instead,
he suggested that Jimi reform a power trio,

00:25:27.730 --> 00:25:30.600
as this was a more popular -- and more profitable
-- line-up.

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:36.169
Jimi was clearly not happy, as he preferred
larger ensembles, which allowed for more experimentation

00:25:36.169 --> 00:25:41.120
and improvisation. The following stent, with
Billy Cox and Buddy Miles, ended with a disastrous

00:25:41.120 --> 00:25:46.899
concert at the Madison Square Garden in New
York, on January 28, 1970. Jimi played only

00:25:46.899 --> 00:25:51.059
two songs, before he sat down on the stage
and refused to continue. Despite Buddy’s

00:25:51.059 --> 00:25:56.500
best efforts, Jimi unplugged his guitar and
walked backstage. Miles, Cox and Hendrix parted

00:25:56.500 --> 00:25:59.879
ways in the aftermath.
Jimi later told to Rolling Stone about his

00:25:59.879 --> 00:26:04.269
exhaustion, resulting from internal conflict:
“I figure that Madison Square Garden is

00:26:04.269 --> 00:26:09.139
like the end of a big long fairy tale. Which
is great. I think it’s like the best ending

00:26:09.139 --> 00:26:16.659
I could possibly have come up with”.
Burning of the Midnight Lamp

00:26:16.659 --> 00:26:21.860
The following months saw Jimi partially reunite
with the original Experience lineup for some

00:26:21.860 --> 00:26:26.909
jams and recording sessions, but nothing concrete
materialised. He was also preoccupied with

00:26:26.909 --> 00:26:32.580
more personal matters. On March 9, 1970, news
got to him that ex-girlfriend Kathy had just

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:34.970
married.
In a desperate attempt to win her back, Jimi

00:26:34.970 --> 00:26:38.919
flew from New York to London the next day
and implored her to leave her husband Ray

00:26:38.919 --> 00:26:42.799
and to move to New York with him. Hendrix
assured her that he had rid himself of all

00:26:42.799 --> 00:26:47.799
his bad habits, all his bad influences. But
it was futile. He had realised far too late

00:26:47.799 --> 00:26:52.230
that Kathy was the love of his life, and he
had lost her to a life without love.

00:26:52.230 --> 00:26:56.830
Jimi remained in Europe until September, having
reformed the Experience once again, with Cox

00:26:56.830 --> 00:27:01.080
on bass instead of Redding. They had even
played an important gig, the Isle of Wight

00:27:01.080 --> 00:27:04.700
festival, on August 30th.
On September 17, he was back in London, and

00:27:04.700 --> 00:27:08.960
he spent the night with his latest lover,
German painter Monika Danneman. The morning

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:13.629
after, she found Jimi unconscious in his bed,
having choked on his own vomit while asleep.

00:27:13.629 --> 00:27:18.351
He was rushed to St Mary Abbot’s Hospital,
when he was pronounced dead at 11:45am, on

00:27:18.351 --> 00:27:21.720
September 18, 1970.
The police found evidence that he may have

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:25.749
overdosed on sleeping pills, the likely cause
of an accidental death. But not everybody

00:27:25.749 --> 00:27:28.669
agreed.
Eric Burdon, vocalist of The Animals and War,

00:27:28.669 --> 00:27:33.590
was the last to perform with Jimi, at a jam
session on the 16th. Burdon claims that after

00:27:33.590 --> 00:27:37.330
his death, he had found a poem written by
Hendrix in Monika’s apartment. According

00:27:37.330 --> 00:27:41.749
to him, this poem was to be intended both
as Jimi’s spiritual last will and testament,

00:27:41.749 --> 00:27:45.789
as well as his suicide note. Burdon did not
produce the note at the inquest, leading to

00:27:45.789 --> 00:27:49.359
the death being ruled as accidental.
Another more sinister theory came to light

00:27:49.359 --> 00:27:54.049
in June of 2009, after the publication of
‘Rock Roadie’, by James 'Tappy' Wright,

00:27:54.049 --> 00:27:57.149
one of the star's roadies.
Tappy writes how, one year after Jimi’s

00:27:57.149 --> 00:28:01.899
death, a drunken Michael Jeffery had confessed
to murdering the guitarist. He and some accomplices

00:28:01.899 --> 00:28:06.389
had forced down his throat the sleeping pills,
alongside two entire bottles of red wine.

00:28:06.389 --> 00:28:11.419
The motive? Jeffery had taken out a life insurance
worth two million dollars on Jimi Hendrix.

00:28:11.419 --> 00:28:15.929
The star had become too unreliable to tour
and record profitably: he was now worth more

00:28:15.929 --> 00:28:20.999
by being dead, than alive. Jeffery never told
the story to anybody else, dying in a plane

00:28:20.999 --> 00:28:25.190
crash in 1973.
Conspiracy? Maybe, with a kernel of truth

00:28:25.190 --> 00:28:29.129
… a doctor on duty at the time of Jimi’s
death told the Telegraph that Hendrix had

00:28:29.129 --> 00:28:33.840
a stomach full of red wine, but no alcohol
in his blood: evidence that he had died immediately

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:36.980
after swallowing the drink, with no time to
metabolize it.

00:28:36.980 --> 00:28:41.570
I will let you draw your conclusions. Whatever
the causes, Jimi Hendrix left his world too

00:28:41.570 --> 00:28:46.559
early, aged only 27 years, like his early
idol Robert Johnson. Like many other rock

00:28:46.559 --> 00:28:50.990
stars of his age, he was too long a victim
of the schemes of greedy managers, and the

00:28:50.990 --> 00:28:55.919
demands and pressures of fame. Luckily for
all of us, his legacy did not melt into the

00:28:55.919 --> 00:29:11.799
sea, like a castle made of sand.

